{
  "heading": "Accessible views",
  "body": "Every view must work with a screen reader and a keyboard. The build checks each `tac.html` file in `client/pages` and `client/components` and stops with `TY1336` when a view breaks one of these rules:\n\n- An `<img>` has an `alt` text that says what the picture shows. A picture that only decorates the page takes `alt=\"\"` together with `role=\"presentation\"` or `aria-hidden=\"true\"`.\n- A `<button>` and a link (`<a href>`) have a name: text inside them, a picture with an `alt` text, `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`. Text in braces counts, and so do components and slots.\n- An `<input>`, `<select>` and `<textarea>` have a label: the field is inside a `<label>`, a `<label for>` in the same view names its `id`, or it has `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`. A placeholder is not a label. Hidden fields and submit, reset, button and image inputs need none.\n- Headings go down one level at a time in a view, such as `<h2>` then `<h3>`. The first heading of a view may have any level, because a component sits inside a page.\n- No `tabindex` is above 0.\n- Every `aria-*` name is a WAI-ARIA 1.2 name; `aria-hidden`, `aria-disabled` and the other true-or-false attributes are `true` or `false`; `aria-hidden=\"true\"` is not on an element that the keyboard can reach; and a `div`, `span`, `p` or other element without a role has no `aria-label`.\n- An `<iframe>` has a `title` or an `aria-label`.\n\nThe build checks the values that you write in the markup. A value in `:alt=\"...\"` or another `:` attribute comes at run time, so the build accepts it. The page always has `<html lang>`: `en`, or `tac.metadata.defaults.lang` and a route's `lang` in `client/manifest.json`. `ty migrate check` lists every finding. While a project migrates, `tac.accessibility.check: false` in `client/manifest.json` turns the rule off (ADR 0074).",
  "code": {
    "file": "example.html"
  }
}
